Subject: Port: the Korean game online with the other releases' DWC
Upstream-base: a353c8f2dfb34eb08fecd725965a46ea629428e3

The Korean release links a newer DWC and GameSpy (RVL SDK 0x4302_145, May
2009) than the other releases (0x4199_60831, 2007). Their matchmaking is a
different protocol: version 90 against 3, another SB command identifier,
other QR2 keys (dwc_hoststate, dwc_suspend and dwc_groupid against
dwc_mresv) and other match commands. The Korean filter asks for
dwc_mver = 90 and drops every SB command of version 3, so even with one
network version word for every release (tweak patch 0058) a Korean player
never met a player of another release, by matchmaking or as a friend.

With the port's tweaks the Korean game now builds the DWC and GameSpy of the
American and Japanese releases, the newest of the older SDK. Every release
branch of their shared sources and headers (VERSION_R4QK01, and the American
and Japanese ones) sits between dwc/mscharged_older_dwc_begin.h, which hides
VERSION_R4QK01 and shows VERSION_R4QJ01, and dwc/mscharged_older_dwc_end.h,
which puts the Korean release back. Recharged compiles the shared sources in
place of the Korean copies (src/RVL_SDK/dwc/R4QK01, gamespy/*/R4QK01). The
branches include no header of the rest of the SDK, so every other library
of the Korean SDK keeps its own layouts (NETMD5Context, for one, is larger
there): DWC reaches NHTTP, SO, NET, NAND and the others by the same public
functions as in the other releases.

The game's calls to DWC follow in the same way (NetworkLobby: the
matchmaking and friend server calls, the receive callback, the
minimum-entries option and the cancel check); the rest of the Korean game's
online code stays its own.

A Korean player's friend code is now made by CRC-8 like the other releases'
instead of MD5, so it changes once. Saved friend codes of the MD5 kind are
still taken (tweak patch 0059). Only PCs meet online (tweak patch 0058), so
no Korean Wii has to read it.

